WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:06.589
In this video, I'd like to show you an example of a Python testing tool,

2
00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:10.230
which you can find within this project.

3
00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:15.949
It's a good example of how Python can be integrated in, in a practical way.

4
00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:22.390
You also might find it useful in writing scripts going further on in,

5
00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:26.789
especially if you're not using your own IDE.

6
00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:29.629
If you go down into the content browser,

7
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:34.350
you're going to find that there's an EUW underscore Python tester.

8
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.350
Again, this is an editor utility widget.

9
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.710
And if you right-hand click and go run editor utility widget,

10
00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:45.990
you're going to see you get up this kind of small,

11
00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.990
what is sort of a fake IDE.

12
00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:53.630
Now, I find this a lot more useful than the output block.

13
00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.990
So what it has is if you run a script,

14
00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.909
you can just hit run and you can copy to take it out.

15
00:01:01.909 --> 00:01:04.390
And there are a bunch of other settings.

16
00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:10.790
Now, what's useful about this is you can run everything within the editor very quickly.

17
00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:18.230
Secondly, I have a whole load of pre-written code,

18
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.909
which I've already tested.

19
00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:21.750
So as I've got stuff to work,

20
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.989
I've come in here and I've saved them as pieces of code.

21
00:01:24.989 --> 00:01:31.420
So for example, at the bottom, I have take screen shot with timestamp.

22
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.579
And if I clear the box and go add code.

23
00:01:35.579 --> 00:01:41.010
Now, when I hit the run, it will take a screenshot,

24
00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.489
which is quite useful.

25
00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:45.620
But the code, I've all gone away and I figured out.

26
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.819
It also has a couple other bits of functionality.

27
00:01:48.819 --> 00:01:53.379
So at the top, it has the query asset and the query actor.

28
00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:58.420
So if I was to select,

29
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.739
for example, let's see if we've got anything for the player start.

30
00:02:02.739 --> 00:02:04.930
You go query actor.

31
00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:08.129
You can see it's giving us the reference here.

32
00:02:08.129 --> 00:02:14.990
It will also open up a page to the player start so that we can look through it.

33
00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:21.229
So what this works on is all we need to do is select anything within the browser.

34
00:02:21.229 --> 00:02:24.830
So, for example, I think this is a static mesh,

35
00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:31.520
go query actor, and that will bring up the static mesh actor section.

36
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.439
And if I select this, so it's going to give us an idea

37
00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:39.680
of what our path is to the top level asset.

38
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.800
Now, actor is going to query stuff, which is in the viewport.

39
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.159
And then we have the equivalent for asset here.

40
00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:50.960
So, for example, if I select material,

41
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.039
it's going to bring up material along with all of the different sort of settings

42
00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.240
and it will bring up the web page directly.

43
00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:04.240
So that's a sort of useful quick querier

44
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.080
so you can find out what you're working with.

45
00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.280
Combined with that, it's got a whole load of different code here

46
00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.759
that you can just access by pushing add code.

47
00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:18.319
It will give you the different code.

48
00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:20.479
Sometimes for comments, sometimes not.

49
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.129
Okay, so that's the first thing.

50
00:03:24.129 --> 00:03:30.560
And I might use that to demonstrate elements as we're going forward.

51
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.319
And again, this is actually quite a good example of how you might make a tool.

52
00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:37.439
So feel free to click on this button in the top right-hand corner,

53
00:03:37.439 --> 00:03:41.759
which will open up the tool within UMG.

54
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.919
And you can come in here and you can view,

55
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.159
unfortunately not terribly well commented,

56
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.199
an example of putting together a tool.

57
00:03:56.129 --> 00:03:58.050
So I hope you find that useful.

58
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:01.729
We might be referencing this in a couple of the further extra videos.

59
00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.810
But that's it for the Python testing tool.
